Hello Central Park Conservancy team,

My name is Rodrigo Castro, founder of Listening.bio, a working biodiversity-monitoring platform that turns environmental recordings into traceable BirdNET candidate detections, human-reviewed observations, maps, and exportable evidence.

I am requesting written guidance about a small, permission-based Central Park pilot. The proposed scope is intentionally limited: three to five locations chosen with your team, 50 to 100 short recordings over 30 days, and a conservative findings package that clearly separates model candidates from reviewed evidence.

The software works end to end: audio upload, configured BirdNET inference, source and model provenance, detection review, mapping, and CSV/GeoJSON exports. A public-audio validation run correctly identified an American Robin while retaining lower-confidence candidates for review. That test validates the workflow, not ecological performance in Central Park.

Listening.bio would provide the technology workflow, processing, data organization, and pilot report at no software cost during the pilot. I am seeking Conservancy guidance on whether this could support an existing stewardship, research, or environmental-education priority; which permissions and locations would be appropriate; and what evidence would be useful to your team.

This would not be presented as a complete ecological survey. The objective is to test whether low-cost acoustic monitoring can responsibly complement existing work.

If this is potentially relevant, could you reply by email with the appropriate staff contact, required permission process, and any stewardship question that acoustic monitoring might usefully support? I can then send the one-page sampling protocol for written review. The evidence workflow is available at https://listening.bio.

Thank you,

